.TH SLURM-TOP 1 "2025" "slurm-top" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
slurm-top \- terminal dashboard for Slurm allocations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B slurm-top
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B slurm-top
queries Slurm node capacity with
.B scontrol show node -o
and queue information with
.BR squeue --json .
It displays scheduler allocations and pending requests; it does not report
sampled CPU, GPU, or memory utilization.
.PP
When the default
.B top
format writes to a terminal, it opens an alternate-screen interactive UI.
When output is not a terminal, it writes a one-time, pipe-friendly snapshot.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --all
Include non-GPU nodes in node-oriented views.
.TP
.BR --format " " \fItop\fR|\fIjson\fR|\fIgrid\fR|\fIdetail\fR|\fItable
Select an output format. The default is
.BR top .
The compatibility value
.B dashboard
is an alias for
.BR grid .
.TP
.BR --sort " " \fIgpu\fR|\fIcpu\fR|\fImem\fR|\fIjobs\fR|\fIuser
Select the initial summary sort. The default is descending running GPU
allocation.
.TP
.BR --interactive
Require the alternate-screen interactive UI. It requires terminal standard
input and output.
.TP
.BR --watch " " \fIduration
Refresh at the supplied Go duration, such as
.BR 5s .
A zero duration prints once outside the interactive UI.
.TP
.BR --timeout " " \fIduration
Set the timeout for each Slurm query. The default is
.BR 10s .
.TP
.BR -h , " " --help
Show command help.
.SH INTERACTIVE CONTROLS
The summary initially sorts running GPUs descending. Use Up and Down, or the
mouse wheel, to select a user. Right or Enter opens that user's job table.
Left or Escape returns to the summary and preserves its prior sort and selected user.
The menu shows JOBS, GPU, and NODE; the active lower-pane view is highlighted.
The selected user's job table also uses the JOBS menu label. Tab and Shift-Tab
cycle through JOBS, GPU, and NODE. The selected user's jobs open from JOBS with
Right or Enter. JOBS, GPU, and NODE can also be selected by clicking their menu
items. A horizontal rule separates the menu from the
current view. In GPU, directional arrows select a node in the displayed grid;
clicking a node also selects it and opens its scrollable details window. Enter
opens the selected node's window; Escape or clicking outside it closes it, and
the mouse wheel scrolls it. In NODE, Up and Down or the mouse wheel scroll
long content; n and p move five rows.
.PP
Press Up from the first row to focus column headers. In header focus, use Left
and Right to choose a column and Enter to sort it descending; a subsequent
Enter changes the same column to ascending. Clicking a column header also
sorts it. The keys
.BR g ,
.BR c ,
.BR m ,
.BR j ,
and
.B u
select GPU, CPU, memory, running-job, and username summary sorts.
.B r
refreshes and
.B q
or Ctrl-C exits.
.PP
A job table defaults to elapsed time descending. If it exceeds terminal width,
use Left and Right while a row is focused to scroll horizontally. Left at the
left edge returns to the summary.
.SH DISPLAY SEMANTICS
Running allocations use the Slurm
.B tres_alloc_str
field. Pending demand uses
.B tres_req_str
and is shown separately. Capacity is derived from
.BR scontrol .
CPU-per-GPU and memory-per-CPU values are ratios of scheduler allocations.
Memory table values are decimal GB; aggregate memory summaries are decimal TB.
.PP
Job details include QoS, partition, progress, and elapsed/requested time.
Elapsed time is formatted as
.BR percent% " [" elapsed "|" requested "]" .
It is a schedule-time estimate, not measured completion. H200 and H200-MIG
bars use typed GPU TRES; generic or other GPU types are not included in those
typed bars.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, disables supported terminal colors.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR
Set to
.B 0
to disable supported terminal colors.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/slurm-top
System package installation path.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/slurm-top/README.md
Package documentation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR squeue (1),
.BR scontrol (1)
